WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.340
What do polls tell us about sexual
harassment? When women are asked whether

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:13.549
they have ever experienced harassment,
polls give us responses ranging from a

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:19.760
low of 42 percent in one poll to a high
of 60 percent in another. Fewer, around 30

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.690
percent of women in recent polls, say
that they have been sexually harassed at

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:28.160
work. There is positive news in a new
survey: A large majority think their

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.210
workplaces take the issue very seriously.
That's important because around

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.870
two-thirds believe that recent
allegations against high profile men

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:41.269
indicate a widespread problem in society,
and not isolated incidences. For the most

00:00:41.269 --> 00:00:44.690
part, men and women agree on what
constitutes harassment, though in most

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.800
cases men are less likely to call a
behavior harassment than women are. Lewd,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.999
aggressive, or threatening behavior is
clearly seen as harassment; flirtatious

00:00:52.999 --> 00:00:57.889
actions less so. When an issue catches
fire like this one has, it's important to

00:00:57.889 --> 00:01:02.120
take care in interpreting early poll
results, but the polls released thus far

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.220
present a fairly consistent picture of
attitudes about harassment.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.140
What do you think about recent news stories about
sexual harassment? Are they isolated

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.340
incidents, or are they more widespread?
Let us know your comments. Also let us

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.329
know what other topics you'd like our
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be

00:01:20.329 --> 00:01:25.810
sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

